From 58e0f18c2027f4661abd7560842cca0ffdac4265 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luc3as <luc3as@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 18:08:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/25] Market memory, retail cache and buyer feedback for the
 agent
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

"Is this a bargain?" cannot be answered from the retail price alone: what a
thing costs new says little about what it sells for used. The search's own
page says that — ninety-six price points every check — and until now they
were thrown away unless they passed the filters.

Every listing on the page is now recorded in a market table (price, size,
condition, hearts, views, first and last seen), at no extra requests. The
webhook payload gains a "market" block per listing: price percentiles over
thirty days, the listing's own percentile, the median for the same
condition, and the median price of listings that vanished within a day —
the closest thing to a sold price the catalog offers. Demand travels too:
favourites, views, minutes since listing, favourites per hour.

Two feedback loops close as well. A verdict's model and retail price are
cached per search and sent as "known_retail" next time, so the same product
does not cost another web search and gets the same number. And 👍/👎 buttons
under each judged alert (and each late-verdict follow-up) store the buyer's
opinion, sent back as "buyer_feedback" — examples of what this particular
buyer calls a deal. Migration 0010 adds the three tables.
